{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterclass in storytelling, direction, and performance, turning the rise of facebook into a gripping, morally complex drama that feels as urgent today as it did upon its 2010 release. directed by david fincher with surgical precision, the film doesn’t just chronicle the creation of a social media empire—it dissects the loneliness, ambition, and betrayal that fuel innovation in the digital age.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him not as a villain or hero, but as a brilliant, socially awkward prodigy whose emotional detachment becomes both his greatest asset and his fatal flaw. his rapid-fire dialogue, delivered with a nervous energy that borders on robotic, captures the mindset of someone who thinks in code but struggles to understand human connection. the supporting cast is equally exceptional: andrew garfield as edwardo saverin brings a quiet heartbreak to his role as the loyal friend turned traitor, while jesse plemons and armie hammer, as the winklevoss twins, are astonishing in their dual portrayal—using motion capture and digital effects to create two distinct yet eerily identical characters who embody privilege and entitlement.\n\nthe screenplay by aaron sorkin is a tour de force. every line crackles with wit and tension, transforming legal depositions and boardroom meetings into high-stakes verbal duels. sorkin’s signature rapid-fire dialogue isn’t just clever—it’s the heartbeat of the film, mirroring the relentless pace of startup culture and the way language becomes a weapon in the battle for ownership and identity.\n\ndanny cohen’s cinematography is stark and cool, using a muted palette that reflects the sterile environment of tech innovation, while trent reznor and atticus ross’s iconic score pulses with electronic unease, turning every scene into a psychological thriller. the editing, especially in the courtroom sequences, is razor-sharp, building suspense not through action but through the weight of words and silences.\n\nbeneath the surface, the film is a meditation on isolation in the age of connection. facebook was built to bring people together, yet the people who built it are profoundly alone. the final scene—zuckerberg refreshing his ex-girlfriend’s facebook page, hoping for a response that never comes—is haunting in its simplicity and devastating in its truth.\n\nthe social network isn’t just a movie about facebook. it’s about the cost of genius, the ambiguity of success, and how the tools we create to connect often end up alienating us from each other. it’s sharp, smart, and unforgettable—a modern classic that only grows more relevant with time.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00014733182615600526}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0005539313424378633}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.2500340938568115}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -1.2248547077178955}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " storytelling", "logprob": -0.4128168225288391}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -2.253030106658116e-05}, {"token": " direction", "logprob": -0.02488912083208561}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " performance", "logprob": -0.3203960359096527}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.16082891821861267}, {"token": " turning", "logprob": -2.0839481353759766}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.06357072293758392}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -0.42259135842323303}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0011703077470883727}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.005251185968518257}, {"token": " a", "logp